{"qid":"Q16","names":["Canada"],"title":"Canada","endonym":{"native":"Canada","lang":"en"},"capsule":"Canada became a self-governing Dominion within the British Empire on 1 July 1867 under the British North America Act, uniting the provinces of Ontario, Quebec, Nova Scotia, and New Brunswick; subsequent westward expansion brought Manitoba (1870), British Columbia (1871), Prince Edward Island (1873), Alberta and Saskatchewan (1905), and Newfoundland (1949). The Statute of Westminster (1931) granted Canada full legislative independence, and the Constitution Act (1982) patriated the constitution and added the Charter of Rights and Freedoms. Canada remains a constitutional monarchy and parliamentary democracy governed under a federal structure, with its territorial footprint in our dataset attested from 1932.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Canadian provincial and territorial boundaries are among the most precisely documented of any state, established by statute and surveyed; the 1932 dataset start omits earlier Dominion-era geometry, but extant boundaries are modern survey-grade.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Canada - Exploration, Confederation, Multiculturalism","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Canada/History"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Canada","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canada"}],"britannica":"place/Canada","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Canada","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"R. B. Bennett","nativeName":"R. B. Bennett","roman":"R. B. Bennett","start":1930,"end":1935,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":"Q128633","name":"William Lyon Mackenzie King","nativeNa